  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe very persistent low pressure system loitering in the Pacific \nNorthwest will finally be ejecting eastward on Monday and Tuesday. \nThe models are trending towards a slightly more eastward and \namplified evolution of this low's passage, possibly influenced by a \nstronger shortwave just ahead of it. Moisture content ahead of this \nsystem seems to be on track with previous forecasts. PWAT values are \nrunning about 2 to 3 standard deviations above climatology. The few \nCAMs that go out to Monday evening do not show much in the way of \nrainfall with most of the synoptic forcing staying well north of the \narea. Regardless a few showers and storms from the afternoon will \nlinger through the early evening. Tuesday the western most lobe of \nthe upper level system will swing southeast to pass just north of \nour area. With it a weak cold front will pass through. There is \nenough moisture ahead of the front that a few storms are possible, \nparticularly for the southern NE Panhandle areas. No severe weather \nis anticipated at this time for this round of storms before they \ndepart to our east. The front should cut afternoon highs by about 5 \ndegrees, so that is nice. What isn't nice are the low humidity and \ngusty winds behind it which could elevate fire weather conditions.\n\nPost cold front Wednesday is also slightly cooler and drier. \nThe ridge that has been patiently waiting for its chance to \nbuild back west will do so by Thursday. Downsloping flow from \nthe Intermountain West will act to heat us back up and keep us \ndry. Continued gusty winds does mean fire weather will be the \ntopic of upcoming forecast. The type of pattern will continue \nuntil our next Pacific upper level system arrives in the very \nlong term, hopefully with more rain.",
